  "why": "The statement asks which (m,n) give finite B(m,n), a classification over infinitely many pairs, so each exponent-range result (n=2,4,6 finite; odd/even large n infinite) is a case. The 557 bound is dated to v2 (April 2023); the March 2024 re-postings are editorial, so it is pre-snapshot. Restricted-group results (Kostrikin 1958, Havas-Wall-Wamsley, Vaughan-Lee) are about R(m,n), not B(m,n): variant. The 1981 St Andrews chapter is a conditional reduction: none. The MDPI 2024 paper concerns infinitude of initial approximations, a different object, per its abstract: variant. The cost-one paper studies properties of groups already known to be infinite and settles nothing about finiteness: none. Bachmuth's claims are set aside as not_credible: on the free-group reading they contradict established theorems, they have had no uptake in 10-20 years, and the field still lists B(2,5) as open. The Bar-Ilan page credits no system with any mathematics and gives no result.",
